# Autonomous law study material generator

## Core objective

Generate complete, exam-oriented law study material from **any** arbitrary input directory. The output must be a **self-contained, topper-quality study pack** that a student can use end-to-end — from first learning to last-hour revision — for any law subject at any Indian university.

---

## Phase 1 — Input discovery and classification

### Recursive directory scanning

Always **recursively scan** the entire directory the user provides. Support nested folders, mixed file types, inconsistent naming, duplicate files, and unstructured academic dumps.

Supported file types (extract text where possible; flag unreadable binaries for user OCR):
`pdf`, `md`, `txt`, `docx`, `pptx`, `jpg`, `png`, scanned PDFs, OCR text, `html`.

### Document classification

Before generation, classify all discovered files into these categories:

| Priority | Category | Identification Signals |
|:---:|:---|:---|
| 1 | **Syllabus** | Keywords like "syllabus", "course outline"; unit/module structure; marks distribution; course code; prescribed readings. This is the **master content blueprint**. |
| 2 | **Previous Year Papers (PYQ)** | University/board names; exam layout patterns; question numbering; "attempt any" phrases; semester/year in filename. |
| 3 | **Bare Acts / Statutes** | Statutory drafting: sections, articles, definitions, provisos, explanations, amendments. |
| 4 | **Case Law Material** | Case names, citations, party labels, holdings, ratios. |
| 5 | **Reference Material** | Textbooks, notes, guides, presentations, study packs, articles, digests. |
| 6 | **Classroom Notes** | Concise explanations, bullets, OCR of handwriting, lecturer emphasis. |

---

## Phase 2 — Knowledge base construction

After classification, build **one unified legal knowledge base**:

- Merge syllabus topics, references, PYQ signals, statutes, cases, and notes.
- Deduplicate aggressively.
- Preserve legal terminology, statutory wording, case names, and section/article references where verified.
- The **syllabus controls**: module/unit order, topic boundaries, relative depth, and output alignment.
- If no syllabus exists, infer structure from dominant statutes and reference headings; note the inference briefly in `README.md`.

### Subject detection

Infer the subject from syllabus wording, dominant statutes, and vocabulary. Use the inferred label for folder naming and question IDs. Examples: Constitutional Law, IPC, CrPC, CPC, Contract, Tort, Family Law, Jurisprudence, Labour Law, Administrative Law, IPR, Cyber Law, Company Law, International Law.

